tionale**\\n**1. CD44-high is not a defining marker for human memory T cells**\\nIn human peripheral blood, CD44 is expressed at high levels on most CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells, including naïve, central memory, and effector memory populations.  \\nTherefore, CD44-high is not used to distinguish memory subsets in humans.CD44-based naïve vs memory separation is specific to mouse immunophenotyping.\\n\\n**2. CD122-high is characteristic of specific memory-like subsets, not all CD45RO-... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3:   \"comments\": [],\n4:   \"title\": \"[Class hierarchy] Remove CD44-high and CD122-high from CD45RO-positive memory T cells\",\n5:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3454\"\n6: }\n\n(End of file - total 6 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \"**CL term**\\n- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \\n- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \\n\\nThese classes are currently used with *in taxon: Homo sapiens*.\\n\\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\\nFor both parent classes (CL_0001204 and CL_0001203), please remove:\\n1. **CD44-high**  \\n2. **CD122-high**\\nRemoving these markers from the parent classes will correctly update all subclasses inheriting from these CD45RO-positive memory T cell terms.\\n\\n**Improved definitions**\\n**- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive **\\n\\nA CD4-positive, alpha-beta long-lived T cell with the phenotype CD45RO-positive and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\\n\\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224 \\n\\n\\n**- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  **\\n\\nA CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell with memory phenotype indicated by being CD45RO and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\\n\\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\\n- PMID: 24258910\\n- PMID: 21926977  \\n- PMID: 41254224 \\n\\n\\n\\n**Rationale**\\n**1. CD44-high is not a defining marker for human memory T cells**\\nIn human peripheral blood, CD44 is expressed at high levels on most CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells, including naïve, central memory, and effector memory populations.  \\nTherefore, CD44-high is not used to distinguish memory subsets in humans.CD44-based naïve vs memory separation is specific to mouse immunophenotyping.\\n\\n**2. CD122-high is characteristic of specific memory-like subsets, not all CD45RO-... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"[Class hierarchy] Remove CD44-high and CD122-high from CD45RO-positive memory T cells\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3454\"\n}","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__issue_context__.json","time":{"start":1778525808440,"end":1778525808449}},"id":"prt_e18661eaf001Bt9nyhlpRQL4rr","sessionID":"ses_1e79a7e60ffehFBfBoeFyKt5iR","messageID":"msg_e186596c7001vUYfdr60NWW6WT"}}
